Subject: rayeasio: an ASIO driver on PipeWire, registered in every prefix

Music programs (FL Studio, Ableton Live, Reaper) want ASIO for small
buffers. Add "Raye ASIO": a PE driver with a unix side that is a client
of PipeWire's JACK library, loaded by path so a JACK library that needs
its own server is never picked up first.

- The whole duplex cycle runs in one JACK callback; the host's buffer
  switch runs on its own thread, handed each cycle through futexes, with
  realtime priority from RLIMIT_RTPRIO or RealtimeKit. A late host costs
  one cycle of silence and an overload message, never a stuck graph.
- The buffer size the host picks (32 to 2048, 256 by default) is forced
  on the graph while the driver runs (node.force-quantum); other programs
  keep playing at that size, as with ASIO on Windows.
- Channels follow the default output and input, linked through PipeWire's
  "system:" names and moved within a second when the default changes.
  Links the user makes by hand are left alone.
- Built for 64-bit and 32-bit programs; the unix call structures have the
  same layout on both, so WoW64 needs no thunks.
- The control panel (EN/FR) picks the buffer size and asks the host to
  restart its audio.

wine.inf registers it in every new or updated prefix, under
HKLM\Software\ASIO\Raye ASIO. The configure hunk is the one autoconf
writes for a new dll, so the build does not need autoreconf.

The ASIO plumbing (thiscall layout, selectors, buffer switch with time
info) follows WineASIO (LGPL 2.1 or later); the transport, threading,
registration and panel are new.
